H64_EXPLAINER = [
 ('Question', "When a tagger sees a heavier-looking jet, does it respond to the jet's invariant mass $m$ or to how wide the jet looks? For a two-prong decay the opening angle is $\\Delta R\\approx 2m/p_T$, so mass and width are tied at fixed $p_T$ but come apart under a boost.", None),
 ('Physics background', "A Lorentz boost along the jet direction leaves $m^2=E^2-|\\vec p|^2$ unchanged, multiplies $|\\vec p_J|$ by $\\lambda$, and shrinks constituent angles to the axis by about $1/\\lambda$. A network that computes $m$ does not move. One that computes the angular size at fixed $p_T$ normalisation, or $m/p_T$, moves as if the jet were narrower. A network that reads $m$ and $p_T$ separately moves as under a $p_T$ change at fixed mass.", None),
 ('Method', "Real jets from hls4ml (10,000), JetClass (20,000, random rows), Top Landscape (4,000) and quark–gluon (4,000). Edits at $\\lambda\\in\\{0.8,0.9,1.1,1.25\\}$: $B$ = exact boost ($m$ fixed to $4\\cdot10^{-12}$), $P$: $p^\\mu\\to\\lambda p^\\mu$, $A$: $(\\Delta\\eta,\\Delta\\phi)\\to(\\Delta\\eta,\\Delta\\phi)/\\lambda$ at fixed $p_T$, $R$: a rotation about the axis of equal displacement. Per class $k$: $\\Delta_{X,k}=\\langle l_k(X)-l_k(0)\\rangle_{y=k}$ with $l_k=\\log p_k-\\log(1-p_k)$; $R_B=\\Delta_B/\\Delta_A$ (0 = invariant-mass reader, 1 = angular-size or $m/p_T$ reader). 16 networks, untrained and weight-permuted nulls, reference MLPs on $(m,p_T)$, $m/p_T$ and $(\\Delta R_{\\rm prongs},p_T)$. Holm over 284 tests per family.", None),
 ('Result', "Per class at $\\lambda=0.9$ ($B$ / $P$ / $A$): JEDI-linear W $-3.62$ / $-3.06$ / $-5.34$ (rotation $+0.01$); JetClass ParT_kin W $-0.13$ / $-0.43$ / $-1.21$; Top ParT top $-0.16$ / $-0.61$ / $-0.87$. Class-weighted $R_B^w$ for $\\lambda<1$: JetClass / Top ParT and ParticleNet 0.09–0.45, hls4ml 0.61–1.19, QG ParT / PN 0.58–0.86; MLP($m/p_T$) 0.97–1.00. Outside the training $p_T$ range at $\\lambda=0.9$: JetClass 36 %, Top 67 %, QG 100 %.", 'results/physics/h64_wjet_boost.png'),
 ('Checks', "Unedited accuracies reproduce known values (JEDI 80.66 %, ParT_full 85.83 %, Top ParT 93.70 %). Rotation control changes 0.4–1.4 % of calls. $p_T$-reweighted C2ST of boosted vs real jets 0.53–0.57 on JetClass / Top. MLP($m,p_T$), which has no angular input, gives $R_B$ from −2.9 to 1.7, and on hls4ml the same pattern as JEDI-linear.", 'results/physics/h64_RB.png'),
 ('Physical significance', "Large JetClass and Top taggers respond to a mass-preserving boost much less than to an equal narrowing at fixed $p_T$, so they do not read the jet's angular size alone. A large boost response in the hls4ml taggers is matched by a classifier on mass and $p_T$, so it does not by itself show angle reading (see H64v).", None),
]

