H67_EXPLAINER = [
 ('Question', "Does a jet tagger read the jet's invariant mass $m$, or its angular width together with its $p_T$? A boost along the jet axis (H64) moves width and $p_T$ together at fixed $m$, so it could not tell these apart.", None),
 ('Physics background', "For a two-prong decay with prong momentum fractions $z$ and $1-z$ and opening angle $\\Delta$: $$m\\simeq p_T\\,\\Delta\\sqrt{z(1-z)},\\qquad w=\\frac{\\sum_i p_{T,i}\\,\\Delta R_i}{\\sum_i p_{T,i}}\\simeq 2z(1-z)\\,\\Delta\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\frac{w}{m/p_T}\\simeq 2\\sqrt{z(1-z)}.$$ So at fixed $m$ and $p_T$ the width can still change, but only by changing how the momentum is shared between the prongs. That also changes $D_2=e_3/e_2^3$ and $\\tau_{21}=\\tau_2/\\tau_1$, which are larger for lopsided, QCD-like jets.", None),
 ('Method', "Each jet is split into 2 exclusive-$k_T$ subjets (3 for top-class jets). Two exact knobs: $f$ moves $p_T$ between the prongs at fixed prong centroids and fixed $\\sum p_T$; $u$ scales each prong's distance from the axis. The jet is then re-centred on its E-scheme axis and rescaled to its original $p_T$, and a per-jet Newton solve finds $(f,u)$ for $E_w$ ($w\\times0.9$ at fixed $m$, $p_T$) and $E_m$ ($m\\times1.1$ at fixed $w$, $p_T$); $m$, $p_T$, $\\eta$, $\\phi$ hold to $10^{-7}$. Samples: hls4ml 2,000 per class (seed 6701), JetClass W/Z/top/QCD 2,000 random rows each (rng 6702), Top Landscape 5,000 + 5,000 (rng 6703). 13 networks, permuted copies, and reference classifiers MLP($m,p_T$), MLP($w,p_T$), MLP($m,w,p_T$), MLP($m_A,p_T$), GBDT(EFP). With $\\Delta s$ the change of the own-class log-odds, $$\\varphi=\\frac{\\sum|\\Delta s(E_w)|}{\\sum|\\Delta s(E_w)|+\\sum|\\Delta s(E_m)|}$$ is 0 for a mass reader and 1 for a width + $p_T$ reader. Control crot: each prong rotated about its own centroid at fixed $m$, $w$, $p_T$.", None),
 ('Result', "JetClass ParT_full W $\\varphi=0.350\\,[0.342,0.357]$, top 0.416; Top Landscape taggers on top jets 0.43–0.47; references MLP($m,p_T$) 0, MLP($w,p_T$) 1, MLP($m_A,p_T$) ≤ 0.05; hls4ml JEDI-linear W 0.116, Z 0.225, top 0.425. Under $E_w$ (less equal sharing) the own-class score falls (ParT_full W $-1.44$, top $-3.55$). Figure: $\\varphi$ per network and reference (0 = mass reader, 1 = width + $p_T$ reader; a sharing-only reader sits near 0.5, H67v).", 'results/physics/h67_phi.png'),
 ('Checks', "Unedited accuracies reproduce (JEDI-linear 0.813, ParT_full W 0.78, Top ParT 0.943). About 70 % of JetClass jets and 50 % of Top jets converge for the pair used; non-converged jets are excluded. Permuted networks give $\\varphi\\approx0.5$ with 5–20× smaller responses. $E_w$ raises $D_2$ by 0.3–0.6 and $\\tau_{21}$ by about 0.03 at fixed mass. Figure: a real W jet before and after $E_w$ and $E_m$; the prong share changes the same way in both edited panels.", 'results/physics/h67_jet.png'),
 ('Physical significance', "At fixed mass and momentum, the JetClass and Top taggers respond to how evenly the jet's momentum is shared between its prongs, as a signal decay (symmetric) differs from a QCD splitting (lopsided). Whether the quantity they use is the width, the sharing, $D_2$ or $\\tau_{21}$ is not separated (see H67v).", None),
]

