H58_EXPLAINER = [
 ('Question', "When a network's yes/no calls change under the same physics edits as a classical tagger's, which classical tagger is it most like: a soft-drop (groomed) mass window with an N-subjettiness cut, the groomed-mass window alone, or an ungroomed mass window? And does the answer hold when every tagger keeps the same 50 % of signal jets?", None),
 ('Physics background', "Soft drop ($\\beta=0$, $z_{\\rm cut}=0.1$) removes soft wide-angle branches, so the groomed mass $m_{SD}$ ignores soft radiation that the ungroomed mass $m$ picks up. $\\tau_{21}=\\tau_2/\\tau_1$ and $\\tau_{32}=\\tau_3/\\tau_2$ measure how two- or three-pronged a jet is. A tagger's working point is the cut that defines its call; a wide window that keeps most signal changes its call less often under a small edit, so taggers must be compared at the same signal efficiency.", None),
 ('Method', "The nine H55 edits (collinear splits, a soft particle, an axis change and its random-direction control, three soft particles that soft drop keeps (gin) or removes (gout), a decay-angle rotation at fixed jet four-momentum, a beam boost) on fresh rows: hls4ml 3,000 per class, JetClass 3,000 random rows per class, Top Landscape and quark/gluon 3,000 per class. Every tagger and network at 50 % signal efficiency (@50). Fingerprint $f=(\\mathrm{chg}_e,\\mathrm{flow}_e)_e$: share of calls that change and net call flow per edit, each scaled by its RMS. Similarity $\\cos(f_a,f_b)=f_a\\cdot f_b/(|f_a||f_b|)$. Contrasts: $d_{\\rm shape}=\\cos(f_{\\rm net},f_{m_{SD}+\\tau})-\\cos(f_{\\rm net},f_{m_{SD}})$, $d_{\\rm groom}=\\cos(f_{\\rm net},f_{m_{SD}})-\\cos(f_{\\rm net},f_{m})$. Paired jet bootstrap $B=5\\times10^4$, Holm over 96 tests; 55 weight-permuted copies per architecture.", None),
 ('Result', "Top tasks: $d_{\\rm groom}=+0.10$ to $+0.18$ on JetClass (4/4, Holm ≤ 0.04) and $+0.08$ to $+0.14$ on Top Landscape; $d_{\\rm shape}$ consistent with 0 except the Top EFN ($+0.27$). JetClass Z: $d_{\\rm shape}=+0.05$ to $+0.07$. hls4ml W/Z: $d_{\\rm groom}<0$ for Deep Sets, JEDI-linear and the JEDI-style float network, $>0$ for the Transformer. At published cuts every network looks closer to the shape taggers, so H55's preference for shape taggers depended on the working point.", 'results/physics/h58_contrasts.png'),
 ('Checks', "Unedited accuracies within 0.02 of known values (JEDI-linear 0.806, JetClass ParT_full top 0.956, Top ParT 0.942). IRC-safe taggers change no calls under splits; the mass window none under the decay rotation. Rows disjoint from H55. Rank-p tests against the permuted copies cannot pass Holm at 55 copies over 162 cells. Observable changes below float precision were set to 0 after a first pass (moves JetClass values by < 0.003). The verifier's controls are in the next entry.", None),
 ('Physical significance', "H58 read this as \"top taggers follow the groomed mass, not the ungroomed mass\". The verifier shows that this reading does not hold (next entry).", None),
]

